# The lit flake, as a literate program

This page is the build file for lit. It is a literate program. lit tangles this
page to make the `flake.nix` file in the root of the repository. The two files
are the same, byte for byte. A CI check tangles the page again on each run and
compares the result. This makes sure that the two files stay the same.

This page is the first step in the self-host path of lit (SPEC.md §13). The Nix
part of the toolchain is now a lit document. lit has a Nix grammar for syntax
highlighting (§5.5). The grammar gives color to this page.

A flake is one attribute set. This flake is one root chunk. The root chunk has a
`description` and a pinned `nixpkgs` input. It also has an `outputs` function.
The `outputs` function starts with a `let` block of two shared helpers. Then it
gives the `packages`, `devShells`, and `checks`.
<<flake.nix>>=
{
  description = "lit — a web-first literate programming system built on noweb";

  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }:
    let
      <<system iteration>>

      <<pinned katex assets>>
    in
    {
      packages = eachSystem (system: pkgs: rec {
        <<the lit package>>
        <<the tangled flake output>>
        default = lit;
      });

      devShells = eachSystem (system: pkgs: {
        <<the dev shell>>
      });

      checks = eachSystem (system: pkgs: {
        <<the checks>>
      });
    };
}
@
## System iteration

lit builds each output for the four systems in the list. `eachSystem` is the
helper for this. It maps a function of `system` and `pkgs` across the list with
`genAttrs`. You write `packages`, `devShells`, and `checks` one time. lit then
makes one instance for each system.
<<system iteration>>=
systems = [ "x86_64-linux" "aarch64-linux" "x86_64-darwin" "aarch64-darwin" ];
eachSystem = f: nixpkgs.lib.genAttrs systems (system:
  f system nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${system});
@
## The KaTeX pin

lit renders math at weave time. The HTML from KaTeX is coupled to its own CSS
and font metrics. The assets must match the version of KaTeX that the `katex`
crate contains. The `katex` package in nixpkgs is a different version. A wrong
version makes each equation show two times. The flake pins the version and the
asset bundle together, by hash (SPEC.md §6.3).
<<pinned katex assets>>=
# KaTeX renders to HTML that is version-coupled to its own CSS and font
# metrics, so these assets must match the version the `katex` crate
# embeds (0.16.4) — nixpkgs' katex is a different release. See SPEC.md
# §6.3. Bump both together or math silently mislays.
katexVersion = "0.16.4";
katexAssets = pkgs: pkgs.fetchzip {
  url = "https://github.com/KaTeX/KaTeX/releases/download/v${katexVersion}/katex.tar.gz";
  sha256 = "0pgajj8vqfvgws3wnn5bfy4h3w75n4dz8jvjsxcw8s80jnp0md8c";
};
@
## The package

The package is a `buildRustPackage` with one special step. The pipeline stages
of noweb (`markup`, `nt`, and more) are in `lib/noweb`, not in `bin`. The `lit`
binary needs these stages and the KaTeX assets at run time. `postFixup` wraps
the binary. The wrap puts noweb on `PATH` and sets `LIT_KATEX_ASSETS` to the
assets. The build of the package also runs the hermetic cargo tests.
<<the lit package>>=
lit = pkgs.rustPlatform.buildRustPackage {
  pname = "lit";
  version = "0.1.0";
  src = self;
  cargoLock.lockFile = ./Cargo.lock;
  nativeBuildInputs = [ pkgs.makeWrapper ];
  # noweb's pipeline stages (markup, nt, ...) live in lib/noweb,
  # not bin — see SPEC.md §4.2.
  postFixup = ''
    wrapProgram $out/bin/lit \
      --prefix PATH : ${pkgs.noweb}/bin:${pkgs.noweb}/lib/noweb \
      --set LIT_KATEX_ASSETS ${katexAssets pkgs}
  '';
};
@
## The tangled flake, as an output

This output makes the dogfood loop a file that you can build. `nix build .#flake`
tangles `flake.nix.nw` with the new `lit` binary. It makes the same `flake.nix`
that built this output. The loop is not circular. The build needs `lit`, and
`lit` needs the committed `flake.nix` as its permanent bootstrap (SPEC.md §13).
`checks.dogfood` compares the committed file with this output.

<<the tangled flake output>>=
# `nix build .#flake` re-tangles flake.nix.nw and emits the flake.nix this
# very output was built from — the dogfood loop as a buildable artifact
# (SPEC.md §13). checks.dogfood diffs the committed file against this.
flake = pkgs.runCommand "flake.nix" { } ''
  cd ${self}
  ${lit}/bin/lit tangle flake.nix.nw --out $TMPDIR
  cp $TMPDIR/flake.nix $out
'';
@
## The dev shell

The dev shell has the Rust toolchain and noweb. It makes the same two changes to
the environment that the wrapper makes in the package. With these changes, `cargo
run` in `nix develop` behaves the same as the installed binary.
<<the dev shell>>=
default = pkgs.mkShell {
  packages = with pkgs; [ cargo rustc rustfmt clippy rust-analyzer noweb ];
  shellHook = ''
    export PATH="${pkgs.noweb}/lib/noweb:$PATH"
    export LIT_KATEX_ASSETS="${katexAssets pkgs}"
  '';
};
@
## The checks

`nix flake check` runs the full test set of lit. Each check is an independent
